.TH "TRUSTEDSCRIPT.TOJSON" "3JS" "October 30, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
TrustedScript.toJSON \- TrustedScript: toJSON() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]toJSON()\f[B]\f[R] method of the \f[CR]TrustedScript\f[R]
interface returns a string representing the \f[CR]TrustedScript\f[R]
object.
This is the same string that \f[CR]TrustedScript.toString()\f[R]
returns.
.PP
The method is automatically called by \f[CR]JSON.stringify()\f[R] when a
\f[CR]TrustedScript\f[R] object is encountered during JSON
serialization.
The serialized value can then be converted back using the
\f[CR]TrustedTypePolicy.createScript()\f[R] method within the reviver of
\f[CR]JSON.parse()\f[R], which runs the policy\(cqs transformation on it
again.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
toJSON()
.EE
.SS Parameters
None.
.SS Return value
A string.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Calling toJSON() directly
This example creates a \f[CR]TrustedScript\f[R] object.
Calling \f[CR]toJSON()\f[R] directly returns its stored string value.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] sanitized = scriptPolicy.createScript(\(dqeval(\(aq2 + 2\(aq)\(dq);

\f[B]const\f[R] json = sanitized.toJSON();
console.log(json); \f[I]// Same value as sanitized.toString()\f[R]
console.log(\f[B]typeof\f[R] json); \f[I]// \(dqstring\(dq\f[R]
.EE
.SS Serializing to a JSON string
The \f[CR]TrustedScript\f[R] object from the previous example is
serialized using \f[CR]JSON.stringify()\f[R], which calls the
\f[CR]toJSON()\f[R] method automatically.
.IP
.EX
console.log(JSON.stringify(sanitized));
.EE
.PP
This would log the following JSON string, including the surrounding
double quotes, which are part of the string\(cqs content:
.IP
.EX
\(dqeval(\(aq2 + 2\(aq)\(dq
.EE
